    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"The collection of MPEG movies assembled in this CD ROM package relates to various aspects of fossil-fuel energy, particularly oil and gas. All of the MPEG movies are extracted from two videos produced by the U.S. Geological Survey: \"The Future of Energy Gases\" and \"Wrestling with Uncertainty.\" The MPEG movies assembled here are subdivided into three broad categories: the FIRST SECTION shows examples of geologic settings in both map and cross-sectional views of where petroleum is commonly formed, and displays in animation the maturation, migration and entrapment of petroleum resources. The SECOND SECTION portrays the exploration and extraction history of petroleum. This section includes uses of fossil fuels that exhibit a substitution trend from solids to liquids to gases, a trend in fuels toward greater efficiency and lesser amounts of CO2 per BTU. The latter is a segue into the final section. The THIRD SECTION illustrates various aspects of pollution, particularly the greenhouse-gas phenomena and the emissions of pollutants such as NOx, SOx, and VOC.\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Howell, David G and Swinchatt, Jonathan P and Vigil, Jose F. (1999). USGS OFR 99-21: Animations for petroleum systems; 36 short MPEG movies. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/kr770bj6276",
            "type": "preferred citation",
